`awaitConnectionFinalized()` is implemented as `Effect.runPromise(service.awaitConnectionFinalized)` (`server.ts:488-489`). The package explicitly restricts the Promise facade's runtime calls to creating/building and closing its one scope; server lifecycle work must remain owned by that captured scope. It also asks for lifecycle controls through the narrow server test constructor rather than expanding the production-facing `MusicSessionServer` interface with test-only behavior. Provide the signal through a scoped/injected test hook or boundary Promise created as part of the graph, without an additional `runPromise`.

### High — Post-bind faults remain unobserved through the Promise facade

`startMusicSessionServer` still never evaluates `service.awaitFailure`. A post-bind server error under this facade only enters `serverFaults`; it neither closes the scope nor reaches a returned Promise. The coder result continues to list this as residual work. Integrate fault supervision into the facade's single scoped graph and prove it with deterministic injection.

### High — The required lifecycle and error matrix remains substantially absent

The suite remains seven facade tests and still lacks the narrow Node-operation/lifecycle constructor and comprehensive exact counters. Missing evidence includes direct Layer/provider/source/listener acquisition; complete two-client hello/status/state replay; blocked command/sample closure; acceptance racing shutdown; late-write suppression; connection-defect observation; injected close/unlink/`ENOENT`; cleanup continuation and repeated failed-close outcomes; and signal-handler removal. These are explicit Phase 3 acceptance gates.

